<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Macedonian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">214–<a href="/years/205-bce">205 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Macedonian War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a>, who had allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Rome left most of the fighting to its Greek allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, while Roman fleets raided the coasts, sacking Dyme in 208 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-phoenice">Peace of Phoenice</a> in 205 BCE ended the war without decisive result.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war.jpg" alt="First Macedonian War" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en translator: Manlleus (ca) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mediterranean_at_218_BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was occupied by its war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> saw a chance to extend his power westward. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, he was urged on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-of-pharos">Demetrius of Pharos</a>, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had made ruler of most of coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> before he turned against them and fled to Philip's court in 219 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> in June 217 BCE, Demetrius advised Philip to make peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> and turn to Illyria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. A peace was concluded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a>.</p><p>Philip spent the winter of 217 to 216 BCE building a fleet of 100 small fast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, lembi, of the Illyrian kind. When he heard of a Roman squadron near Apollonia he retreated in panic, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says cost him his best chance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> in 216 BCE Philip sent ambassadors to Hannibal, and they agreed a treaty in the summer of 215 BCE. Roman forces captured the emissaries on their way home and found the terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the late summer of 214 BCE Philip took Oricum with 120 lembi and besieged Apollonia. The Roman propraetor Marcus Valerius Laevinus retook Oricum, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s account a force of 2,000 men under Quintus Naevius Crista entered Apollonia by night and routed Philip's camp. Philip burned his fleet and withdrew over the mountains. In 213 and 212 BCE he advanced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> by land and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lissus</a>, which gave him access to the Adriatic.</p><p>In 211 BCE Laevinus made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> would fight on land and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at sea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> would keep slaves and booty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> would receive any territory taken. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, Elis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and Messenia were allowed to join. In 210 BCE Laevinus and the Aetolians took Phocian Anticyra, and Rome enslaved its inhabitants. Philip fought on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, winning two battles at Lamia in 209 BCE. Attempts at peace by neutral trading states, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Chios, failed. In 208 BCE Philip took Opus when Attalus was caught off guard and barely escaped to his ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The escape of Attalus proved the turning point. Attalus returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prusias-i-of-bithynia">Prusias I of Bithynia</a> moved against it, and Philip resumed the offensive against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>. In 206 BCE they sued for a separate peace on terms set by Philip, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s consent. In 205 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sent Publius Sempronius Tuditanus with 35 ships and 11,000 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrachium</a>. Finding no allies left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and having kept Philip from aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, Rome agreed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-phoenice">Peace of Phoenice</a> in 205 BCE. There had been no decisive engagements, and the war ended in a stalemate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Macedonian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philip V's treaty with Hannibal in 215 BCE drew Rome across the Adriatic in the <b>First Macedonian War</b> (214–205 BCE), and the Second and Third Macedonian Wars turned Rome into the arbiter of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Exposed to the west, Dyme was sacked in 208 BCE by a Roman force during the <b>First Macedonian War</b>, its people enslaved and later ransomed by Philip V of the Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/dyme">Dyme</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Phoenice, ending the <b>First Macedonian War</b> in 205 BCE, was signed there.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li></ul></section>
